A request that is fast in one region and slow in another is the ordinary shape of a
distributed service, and the ordinary mistake is to report one number for all of them.
This report fits three models to the same synthetic latency sample and asks which of
them earns the summary it produces.

The data are generated, not collected: a fixed set of regions, a fixed per-region mean
drawn around a global mean, and a log-normal response time per request. Everything the
report claims is therefore checkable against the generator, which is the point — the
question here is what each modelling choice does to an answer whose truth is known.

The three candidates differ in one respect only:

- **Complete pooling.** One latency parameter for every region.
- **No pooling.** An independent parameter per region, with nothing shared.
- **Partial pooling.** A per-region parameter drawn from a common distribution whose
  own parameters are estimated alongside them [@gelman2007multilevel].

The last of those is the hierarchical model, and the reason to prefer it is not that
it fits best in-sample but that it estimates a sparse region without pretending the
other regions are irrelevant to it.
